by phcorp at 2026-10-08T21:13:11Z

Thanks for this, it's great to see this land in Symfony!

One architectural question: could the generation pipeline serve schema formats other than JSON Schema? The inputs ([TypeInfo](https://symfony.com/doc/current/components/type_info.html), [PropertyInfo](https://symfony.com/doc/current/components/property_info.html), [Serializer](https://symfony.com/doc/current/serializer.html) and [Validator](https://symfony.com/doc/current/validation.html) metadata) aren't JSON-specific. You could imagine the same pipeline producing GraphQL types, TypeScript definitions or even XSD. And if that's a goal, `JsonSchema` might turn out to be a narrow name.

**How the pipeline is built today.** Reading the metadata is already format-neutral: which properties exist, groups, name conversion, required constructor arguments, defaults, definition naming. The output side is pure JSON Schema, though. Every extension point (class resolvers, property providers, enrichers, definition processors) passes JSON Schema arrays around. So JSON Schema already acts as the intermediate model.

**Is anyone asking for other formats?** Not much, as far as I can tell:

- **GraphQL** and **TypeScript** do get generated from PHP metadata ([API Platform's GraphQL `TypeBuilder`](https://github.com/api-platform/core/blob/2bba68f6c9dc83609759c48abdad9bc7c2880c4f/src/GraphQl/Type/TypeBuilder.php), [GraphQLite](https://github.com/thecodingmachine/graphqlite), [spatie/typescript-transformer](https://github.com/spatie/typescript-transformer)), so there's real usage there.
- **XSD**: there's no library that generates XSD from PHP classes, only the reverse, XSD → PHP ([goetas-webservices/xsd2php](https://github.com/goetas-webservices/xsd2php)). An old request to generate XSD + JSON Schema from Config (#29560) was closed for lack of interest.
- **Protobuf** and **Avro** are written by hand in their own formats ([`.proto`](https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/proto3/), [`.avsc`](https://avro.apache.org/docs/1.12.0/specification/)), not derived from classes.
- **AsyncAPI** [message payloads](https://www.asyncapi.com/docs/reference/specification/v3.0.0#message-object) are JSON Schema by default, so this PR already covers them.

**Is JSON Schema a good enough starting point for other formats?** I think so. What JSON Schema has that the others can't express (some unions, `readOnly`/`writeOnly`, formats like uuid or email, `uniqueItems`, `multipleOf`) is a limitation of those target formats, so a format-agnostic model wouldn't solve it either. API Platform already works this way: it derives some GraphQL types from JSON Schema ([`FieldsBuilder::jsonSchemaToGraphQLType()`](https://github.com/api-platform/core/blob/2bba68f6c9dc83609759c48abdad9bc7c2880c4f/src/GraphQl/Type/FieldsBuilder.php#L751)).

So I don't think this PR needs a new abstraction. JSON Schema works fine as the pivot, and other formats can be built as converters on top of `Schema`, outside the component:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph metadata [Metadata, format-neutral]
        typeinfo[TypeInfo]
        propertyinfo[PropertyInfo]
        serializer[Serializer metadata]
        validator[Validator metadata]
    end
    subgraph component [JsonSchema component]
        generator["SchemaGenerator<br/>resolvers · providers · enrichers · processors"]
        schema[("Schema<br/>JSON Schema + Dialect")]
    end
    metadata --> generator --> schema
    schema --> js[JSON Schema 2020-12]
    schema --> oa[OpenAPI 3.0 / 3.1, Swagger 2.0]
    schema -- flatten --> mcp[MCP / symfony/ai tools]
    schema -. converter, outside the component .-> gql[GraphQL SDL]
    schema -. converter, outside the component .-> ts[TypeScript]
    schema -. converter, outside the component .-> xsd[XSD]
```

That also makes `JsonSchema` the right name. The one thing it implies is that [`Schema`](https://github.com/soyuka/symfony/blob/ff1a007526c9b675534bbd67e93b6f172a254c83/src/Symfony/Component/JsonSchema/Schema.php) (and the TypeInfo `Type` already on [`PropertySchema`](https://github.com/soyuka/symfony/blob/ff1a007526c9b675534bbd67e93b6f172a254c83/src/Symfony/Component/JsonSchema/Enricher/PropertySchema.php)) becomes something converters rely on, so it's worth keeping it stable. A small open question: would it help if `Schema` exposed which class each definition came from? A converter could then name its types without having to parse `$ref`.

PS: in [`ValidatedRegistration.schema.json`](https://github.com/soyuka/symfony/blob/ff1a007526c9b675534bbd67e93b6f172a254c83/src/Symfony/Component/JsonSchema/Tests/Fixtures/schema/ValidatedRegistration.schema.json), `username` gets `"default": ""` alongside `minLength: 3` (from `public string $username = ''` with `Length(min: 3)` in [`ValidatedRegistration.php`](https://github.com/soyuka/symfony/blob/ff1a007526c9b675534bbd67e93b6f172a254c83/src/Symfony/Component/JsonSchema/Tests/Fixtures/ValidatedRegistration.php)). That's valid JSON Schema, but the spec [recommends the default be valid against the schema](https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/json-schema-validation#section-9.2). Maybe skip the default when it breaks a constraint?

